
  Bring each worktree's .env files back in step: a key its source has and a file lacks is added,
  a missing file is rebuilt, then the ports and addresses run.toml links and the values wtm owns
  are settled for the worktree. Values come from the strategy each worktree was created with
  (example, main or parent); --from overrides it for one run.

  A key whose value differs from its source is kept unless --overwrite; a key in no source is
  kept unless --prune. Without arguments, the wizard asks which worktrees to reconcile, those out
  of step and the one you stand in checked; unattended, those are reconciled. A worktree is named
  by its branch or its path; --all asks for every worktree.

  Only what wtm writes is shown (the ports and addresses it moves); every other value, secrets
  included, is withheld unless --show-values. A job up in a worktree whose .env changed runs on
  the old values until restarted: the run says which, with the line that restarts it.

  Usage:
    wtm-next env [worktree...] [flags]

  Examples:
    # Pick the worktrees to reconcile
    wtm env

    wtm env feat/login

    # Also settle the values that differ from their source, and drop the keys no source has
    wtm env feat/login --overwrite --prune --yes

    # See the plan first, then apply exactly that plan
    wtm env --all --dry-run
    wtm env --all --expect-fingerprint <hash>

    # For a script or an agent, with the v2 JSON envelope
    wtm env --all --yes --output json

  Flags:
        --all                         Reconcile every worktree
        --ask                         Ask again the questions this repository remembers an answer to, to change or forget it
    -n, --dry-run                     Show the plan and its fingerprint, apply nothing
        --expect-fingerprint string   Apply only if the facts still have this fingerprint, the one --dry-run printed
        --from string                 Where the values come from for this run: example, main or parent; each worktree's own strategy by default
    -h, --help                        help for env
        --isolation string            Switch the worktrees to isolated (ports, compose project and namespaces of their own) or verbatim (the values wtm owns back to the source's); the one each has by default
        --output string               Output format: text or json (default "text")
        --overwrite                   Give a key whose value differs from its source the source's value
        --prune                       Drop a key no source has
        --show-values                 Show every value whole, secrets included; by default only what wtm writes is shown
    -y, --yes                         Run unattended: no question, no confirmation (without worktrees, those out of step and the one you stand in; a conflict kept unless --overwrite, an orphan unless --prune)

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

